1
00:00:43,794 --> 00:00:45,879
<i>NARRATOR: The future...</i>

2
00:00:45,963 --> 00:00:48,340
<i>The polar ice caps
have melted,</i>

3
00:00:48,424 --> 00:00:50,508
<i>covering the earth with water.</i>

4
00:00:51,219 --> 00:00:55,597
<i>Those who survived
have adapted</i>

5
00:00:55,681 --> 00:00:57,515
<i>to a new world.</i>

6
00:02:26,731 --> 00:02:28,231
(GARGLING)

7
00:02:38,075 --> 00:02:39,617
(METAL CLANGING)

8
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:10,268
(WHISTLES)

9
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,230
(SPEAKING HINDl)

10
00:05:26,911 --> 00:05:28,828
Bad luck, English.

11
00:05:30,122 --> 00:05:34,125
But the Slavers are producing
a good grade of poxy these days.

12
00:05:34,835 --> 00:05:38,296
Cost you a handful of dirt
or maybe that wind chime.

13
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:41,674
What are you doing here?
Just waiting.

14
00:05:41,759 --> 00:05:44,761
Take your hand off the sail.

15
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:46,429
Take it off!

16
00:05:47,389 --> 00:05:51,351
I've seen your boat before.
Haven't seen you.

17
00:05:51,936 --> 00:05:53,394
Took it legal.

18
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:58,024
Previous owner was dead
on the tiller when I found it.

19
00:05:58,108 --> 00:06:01,027
You had another hour
before I traded up again.

20
00:06:01,946 --> 00:06:03,613
Just improving my means.

21
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:07,534
Well, I owe you then.
No, thanks.

22
00:06:07,618 --> 00:06:12,121
I got all the supplies I need.
Just came from an atoll.

23
00:06:13,082 --> 00:06:15,750
Eight days east,
if you're interested.

24
00:06:17,711 --> 00:06:20,213
Two Drifters meet, something
needs to be exchanged.

25
00:06:20,297 --> 00:06:23,841
I know the code. But I'll
give you this one for free.

26
00:06:25,761 --> 00:06:27,804
Nothing's free in Waterworld.

27
00:06:28,305 --> 00:06:29,013
Yeah.

28
00:06:29,014 --> 00:06:29,681
Yeah.

29
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:32,684
(MOTORS WHIRRING)

30
00:06:40,317 --> 00:06:41,734
Smokers.

31
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:45,196
Just enough wind
to get away clean.

32
00:06:49,159 --> 00:06:51,077
DRIFTER: Forget the bag.

33
00:06:54,999 --> 00:06:58,918
It's not worth it. You'll never
make it with your sails down.

34
00:06:59,003 --> 00:07:00,211
Hey!

35
00:07:01,046 --> 00:07:02,755
(MIMICKING GOBBLING)

36
00:07:13,517 --> 00:07:14,601
What can I say?

37
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:15,685
(LAUGHS)

38
00:07:35,414 --> 00:07:36,998
Joe, he's going for it.

39
00:07:37,082 --> 00:07:38,791
What the hell?

40
00:07:44,006 --> 00:07:46,049
Never seen that before.

41
00:07:50,012 --> 00:07:52,347
Here we go! Go!
Drive! Drive!

42
00:07:57,394 --> 00:07:59,312
Faster! Faster!

43
00:07:59,396 --> 00:08:00,730
(WHOOPING)

44
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:23,628
Come on! He's gonna get it!
He's gonna get it!

45
00:08:43,023 --> 00:08:45,233
Go!
Get him! Get him!

46
00:08:45,776 --> 00:08:47,527
(CONTINUES SHOUTING)

47
00:09:06,255 --> 00:09:07,463
(GRUMBLES ANGRILY)

48
00:09:14,430 --> 00:09:17,140
No! No!

49
00:09:17,725 --> 00:09:18,975
(GRUNTING)

50
00:09:23,689 --> 00:09:25,148
(SMOKERS SHOUTING)

51
00:09:25,232 --> 00:09:26,607
(MACHINE GUNS FIRING)

52
00:09:28,026 --> 00:09:29,318
(DRIFTER GROANS)

53
00:09:30,362 --> 00:09:32,029
(DRIFTER SCREAMS IN PAIN)

54
00:10:03,061 --> 00:10:05,980
MAN: Inbound! Straight out
from the gates.

55
00:10:13,864 --> 00:10:15,156
(SPEAKING PORTUGREEK)

56
00:10:15,699 --> 00:10:17,033
English.

57
00:10:20,621 --> 00:10:24,123
Flag's down, Drifter.
We got enough traders.

58
00:10:30,214 --> 00:10:31,756
(PEOPLE MURMURING)

59
00:10:31,882 --> 00:10:33,257
WOMAN: What does he have?

60
00:10:38,347 --> 00:10:39,722
Dirt.

61
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:42,016
Open the gates!

62
00:11:21,723 --> 00:11:23,432
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

63
00:11:24,643 --> 00:11:29,355
MAN: Bones to berries.
Veins to vine.

64
00:11:29,439 --> 00:11:33,234
These tendons to trees.
This blood to brine.

65
00:11:33,318 --> 00:11:35,361
Too old she was.

66
00:11:35,445 --> 00:11:39,949
This woman does leave us,
recycled and enshrined

67
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:42,702
in the presence of him
who leads us.

68
00:11:51,753 --> 00:11:53,170
(CHILDREN CHATTERING)

69
00:12:00,929 --> 00:12:02,555
(BOYS WHINING)

70
00:12:02,639 --> 00:12:03,973
BOY: What is that?

71
00:12:09,897 --> 00:12:11,230
You know me?

72
00:12:12,566 --> 00:12:13,983
I know what you are.

73
00:12:14,151 --> 00:12:15,151
Good.

74
00:12:16,153 --> 00:12:18,571
Then you know if you look
for trouble while you're here,

75
00:12:18,655 --> 00:12:20,948
you'll find more than
you can handle.

76
00:12:21,950 --> 00:12:23,618
You got two hours.

77
00:12:26,914 --> 00:12:28,497
I'll only need one.

78
00:12:39,676 --> 00:12:41,719
He's coming over here.

79
00:12:47,184 --> 00:12:48,935
Me.
You each get one

80
00:12:49,019 --> 00:12:51,395
if everything's here
when I get back.

81
00:13:00,197 --> 00:13:01,280
Pure dirt.

82
00:13:01,365 --> 00:13:02,573
(OTHERS EXCLAIMING)

83
00:13:02,658 --> 00:13:04,617
3.2 kilos.

84
00:13:06,912 --> 00:13:09,038
How'd you come by
so much of it?

85
00:13:10,374 --> 00:13:12,875
Another atoll 30 horizons
west of here.

86
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:14,460
Where'd they get it?

87
00:13:15,504 --> 00:13:18,923
They didn't say.
MAN 1: We heard about that place,

88
00:13:19,007 --> 00:13:20,758
that they was all killed.

89
00:13:22,636 --> 00:13:25,012
That's why they didn't say. Smokers?

90
00:13:25,097 --> 00:13:27,598
Maybe Smokers, maybe Slavers.

91
00:13:29,559 --> 00:13:32,853
So what's the word?
We trading or not?

92
00:13:32,938 --> 00:13:37,274
We'll tally it
like pure hydro.

93
00:13:41,071 --> 00:13:44,031
Works out to 62 chits.

94
00:13:46,994 --> 00:13:48,703
I want twice that.

95
00:13:48,912 --> 00:13:50,538
(PEOPLE MURMURING)

96
00:13:52,124 --> 00:13:54,333
HYDROHOLIC: I just want
one sip of hydro!

97
00:13:54,418 --> 00:13:56,377
NORD: No, not till you tell me.

98
00:13:56,461 --> 00:13:59,880
She got inkings on her back.
I seen her.

99
00:13:59,965 --> 00:14:03,426
They say if you read
the marks on the child,

100
00:14:03,510 --> 00:14:06,387
they'll lead you
all the way to Dryland.

101
00:14:06,471 --> 00:14:09,682
Dryland's a myth.
Some still believe.

102
00:14:09,766 --> 00:14:13,060
They say the Smokers
even got an eye out for her.

103
00:14:14,604 --> 00:14:17,857
Well, then, we'd better
keep it to ourselves.

104
00:14:41,798 --> 00:14:43,507
Help you?
Yeah.

105
00:14:44,551 --> 00:14:46,802
Where's the store?
You found it.

106
00:14:52,809 --> 00:14:54,310
You don't have much.

107
00:14:55,812 --> 00:14:57,563
You the man with the dirt?

108
00:15:16,208 --> 00:15:17,458
Skoal.

109
00:15:21,338 --> 00:15:23,005
One more.
Make it two.

110
00:15:23,090 --> 00:15:26,258
A man this rich will buy for
a fellow outwater, I'm sure.

111
00:15:27,427 --> 00:15:28,594
One.

112
00:15:32,015 --> 00:15:35,184
That's an interesting
pair of boots you got.

113
00:15:36,019 --> 00:15:38,187
Trade?
No.

114
00:15:39,189 --> 00:15:40,356
NORD: Shame.

115
00:15:46,071 --> 00:15:47,071
That's her.

116
00:15:47,364 --> 00:15:49,532
HELEN: Enola.
Hey, what are you doing?

117
00:15:50,033 --> 00:15:51,200
No, you can't do that.

118
00:15:51,284 --> 00:15:53,828
I need another piece.
I want to draw some more.

119
00:15:53,912 --> 00:15:57,248
I'll give it to you.
Just stay in the back.

120
00:15:57,332 --> 00:15:59,959
With all those chits,
you could order up a bath.

121
00:16:00,085 --> 00:16:02,586
Have you ever had a freshwater
bath? Why are you talking to me?

122
00:16:02,671 --> 00:16:04,130
Just being friendly.

123
00:16:09,719 --> 00:16:12,805
Is that a tomato plant?
Yeah.

124
00:16:12,889 --> 00:16:14,765
You have a keen eye.

125
00:16:14,850 --> 00:16:16,976
Saw one in a picture.
How much?

126
00:16:23,108 --> 00:16:24,608
Half your chits.

127
00:16:36,329 --> 00:16:38,706
I'll take them, too.
Take what?

128
00:16:39,416 --> 00:16:42,626
You bought everything.
I'll take them shelves.

129
00:16:44,838 --> 00:16:48,507
So, what did you see out there
in your 15 months?

130
00:16:48,592 --> 00:16:51,802
Such as?
An end to all this water.

131
00:16:51,887 --> 00:16:53,429
Asking the wrong person.

132
00:16:53,513 --> 00:16:54,722
BOY: Where did he come from?

133
00:16:54,848 --> 00:16:56,640
The one they buried today? Uh-huh.

134
00:16:56,725 --> 00:16:58,767
She found the only end
there is.

135
00:17:04,566 --> 00:17:06,942
I've got a proposition
for you, Mariner.

136
00:17:08,278 --> 00:17:09,612
I'm not staying.

137
00:17:09,696 --> 00:17:13,699
We're not asking you to.
All we want is your seed.

138
00:17:14,618 --> 00:17:17,870
ELDER: We can look to our own
for impregnation,

139
00:17:17,954 --> 00:17:22,833
but too much of that sort
of thing gets undesirable.

140
00:17:23,335 --> 00:17:24,919
When she's pregnant,

141
00:17:25,003 --> 00:17:28,088
you go on your way with
all the supplies you need.

142
00:17:28,924 --> 00:17:32,134
You don't have anything.
You're dying.

143
00:17:37,182 --> 00:17:41,268
No man stays out that long
and turns down a woman.

144
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:46,899
He's hiding something.
Maybe he's a Smoker spy.

145
00:17:49,402 --> 00:17:51,111
(WHISTLING)

146
00:17:57,494 --> 00:18:01,580
When the elders say so,
you can leave, and not before.

147
00:18:10,507 --> 00:18:11,882
(GRUNTING)

148
00:18:21,518 --> 00:18:22,726
Gills.

149
00:18:24,521 --> 00:18:26,105
Mutation!

150
00:18:26,189 --> 00:18:28,565
He's a mutant!

151
00:18:28,650 --> 00:18:29,650
(PEOPLE SHOUTING)

152
00:18:30,110 --> 00:18:31,610
MAN: (GROANING) My hand!

153
00:18:35,115 --> 00:18:36,323
(SCREAMING)

154
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:42,288
(PEOPLE CLAMORING)

155
00:18:46,543 --> 00:18:48,127
MAN 2: Throw the nets!

156
00:19:12,819 --> 00:19:14,695
MAN 3: He's a murderer!

157
00:19:18,533 --> 00:19:20,034
(GRUNTING)

158
00:19:21,244 --> 00:19:22,661
By what right...

159
00:19:24,914 --> 00:19:26,999
You pay me to keep the peace.

160
00:19:28,585 --> 00:19:30,169
This isn't it.
No!

161
00:19:30,253 --> 00:19:33,422
He has killed one of ours.
He was defending himself.

162
00:19:33,506 --> 00:19:34,923
He needs to be destroyed!

163
00:19:35,050 --> 00:19:37,468
Kill him. Let's kill him. That may be,

164
00:19:37,552 --> 00:19:40,929
but not here
and not like this.

165
00:20:13,338 --> 00:20:14,588
BOY 1: What is it?

166
00:20:15,131 --> 00:20:16,465
BOY 2: Damn.

167
00:20:17,759 --> 00:20:20,969
They're too tight.
I can't get it off.

168
00:20:24,516 --> 00:20:26,058
Open the gate.

169
00:20:41,574 --> 00:20:43,534
(HUMMING)

170
00:20:59,384 --> 00:21:00,843
Is it a map?

171
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:04,805
You'd tell me if you knew,
wouldn't you, Enola?

172
00:21:04,889 --> 00:21:06,140
Mmm-hmm.

173
00:21:09,769 --> 00:21:12,396
What are you drawing?
I don't know.

174
00:21:13,857 --> 00:21:17,985
Helen, look. Look at the
prodigal child's latest vision.

175
00:21:20,989 --> 00:21:21,989
(CHUCKLES)

176
00:21:24,367 --> 00:21:25,909
How soon
before we can leave?

177
00:21:25,994 --> 00:21:29,288
I don't have any idea yet
where we can go.

178
00:21:29,372 --> 00:21:32,583
I haven't figured out
the tattoo on her back.

179
00:21:32,667 --> 00:21:35,085
I'm a stupid man.
The answer's right there.

180
00:21:35,170 --> 00:21:38,130
It's just beyond me.

181
00:21:41,301 --> 00:21:42,926
Maybe he knows.

182
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:56,023
Yes, I see. They are webbed.

183
00:21:56,900 --> 00:22:01,069
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven,
eight, nine, ten digits. That's wonderful.

184
00:22:01,154 --> 00:22:04,698
They tell me, too, that you
have gills. Isotropic gills.

185
00:22:04,782 --> 00:22:07,743
Are they merely vestigial
or are they functional?

186
00:22:08,369 --> 00:22:12,247
My name is Gregor. I've just
come to pay you a little...

187
00:22:13,625 --> 00:22:14,750
Oh, my God.

188
00:22:15,043 --> 00:22:16,585
(GREGOR LAUGHING)

189
00:22:17,462 --> 00:22:20,756
I'm sorry. I'm not laughing
at you. I'm excited.

190
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:22,966
Your gills,
they're functional.

191
00:22:23,051 --> 00:22:26,428
<i>Ichthyus sapien.
You can breathe in the water.</i>

192
00:22:26,513 --> 00:22:28,305
How deep can you go?

193
00:22:28,389 --> 00:22:33,352
Look, I'm only here because I
want to learn more about you.

194
00:22:38,608 --> 00:22:41,568
You don't like humans
very much, do you?

195
00:22:42,612 --> 00:22:44,571
I can't say that I blame you.

196
00:22:44,656 --> 00:22:48,033
But tell me, are all of your
kind as bad-tempered as you are?

197
00:22:51,913 --> 00:22:54,498
I have no kind.
Oh, fish rot.

198
00:22:54,582 --> 00:22:57,459
I'd be surprised if there weren't
others, and if there aren't,

199
00:22:57,544 --> 00:22:59,294
there will be eventually.

200
00:23:01,172 --> 00:23:04,550
Anyway, I've come here because
I need to ask you a question.

201
00:23:04,634 --> 00:23:08,887
Where did your dirt come from?

202
00:23:08,972 --> 00:23:10,806
Is it from Dryland?

203
00:23:10,890 --> 00:23:13,141
Do you know what this is?

204
00:23:15,311 --> 00:23:17,688
The ancients, they did
something terrible, didn't they?

205
00:23:17,772 --> 00:23:21,525
To cause all this water.
Hundreds, hundreds of years ago.

206
00:23:21,609 --> 00:23:24,069
If I tell you,
will you open this lock?

207
00:23:26,948 --> 00:23:28,365
I haven't a key.

208
00:23:29,826 --> 00:23:32,744
There's a mooring
cleat down there. What?

209
00:23:32,829 --> 00:23:34,079
Good as any key.

210
00:23:34,163 --> 00:23:35,789
A mooring cleat?

211
00:23:37,709 --> 00:23:40,502
I won't hurt anyone.
I'll just leave.

212
00:23:42,005 --> 00:23:44,965
I'll be right back.
MAN: Gregor!

213
00:23:45,049 --> 00:23:48,260
What's your business there?
Move along!

214
00:23:48,344 --> 00:23:50,095
I'm not a brave man.

215
00:23:50,179 --> 00:23:54,516
If you know anything about
Dryland, please tell me. Please!

216
00:23:55,351 --> 00:23:57,436
Don't let it die with you.

217
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:00,022
No, no, no, no. Stop!

218
00:24:00,690 --> 00:24:03,692
MAN: Gregor! Move along!

219
00:24:38,603 --> 00:24:43,398
ELDER: After considerable
deliberation of the evidence at hand,

220
00:24:44,525 --> 00:24:49,488
it is our decision that this mute-o
does, indeed, constitute a threat.

221
00:24:50,573 --> 00:24:53,700
Therefore, in the interest
of public safety,

222
00:24:53,785 --> 00:24:57,454
he is hereby sentenced
to be recycled

223
00:24:57,538 --> 00:24:59,581
in the customary fashion.

224
00:25:00,625 --> 00:25:01,917
Proceed.

225
00:25:08,341 --> 00:25:09,633
I'm sorry.

226
00:25:14,889 --> 00:25:19,184
Bones to berries.
Veins to vines.

227
00:25:19,268 --> 00:25:23,313
These tendons to trees.
This blood to brine.

228
00:25:23,690 --> 00:25:26,525
Too strange for life he was.

229
00:25:26,609 --> 00:25:30,487
This mute-o now does leave us,

230
00:25:30,571 --> 00:25:33,490
recycled and entombed,

231
00:25:33,574 --> 00:25:33,865
in the presence of him
who leads us.

232
00:25:33,866 --> 00:25:37,119
in the presence of him
who leads us.

233
00:25:47,213 --> 00:25:51,591
Smokers! Get out of the sun!

234
00:25:55,221 --> 00:25:57,973
To your posts!

235
00:26:10,653 --> 00:26:12,487
(PEOPLE CLAMORING)

236
00:26:13,156 --> 00:26:15,323
WOMAN: Oh, my God!
Oh, my God!

237
00:26:26,294 --> 00:26:28,962
Kill!
There!

238
00:26:29,130 --> 00:26:30,756
(SHOUTING)

239
00:26:39,307 --> 00:26:42,309
We are safe behind this wall.

240
00:26:43,603 --> 00:26:44,770
Aren't we?

241
00:26:53,696 --> 00:26:55,030
Let me out!

242
00:26:55,782 --> 00:26:57,365
Let me out! I'll fight!

243
00:27:06,584 --> 00:27:08,376
(SMOKERS SHOUTING)

244
00:27:27,772 --> 00:27:29,815
Give me the key
to the city now.

245
00:27:33,694 --> 00:27:35,946
Let's go! Let's go! Let's go!

246
00:27:38,741 --> 00:27:41,910
SMOKER 1: Floor it! Floor it!
SMOKER 2: Come on!

247
00:27:57,844 --> 00:27:58,969
(WOMAN SCREAMING)

248
00:28:02,807 --> 00:28:05,433
Swab it! Swab it!

249
00:28:05,518 --> 00:28:08,103
God damn! More ammo!

250
00:28:23,202 --> 00:28:24,661
SMOKER: Break down the door!

251
00:28:25,580 --> 00:28:26,663
(SCREAMS)

252
00:28:28,958 --> 00:28:30,709
My God!

253
00:28:33,045 --> 00:28:34,045
(GRUNTING)

254
00:28:37,717 --> 00:28:40,010
We'll have this atoll
in no time.

255
00:28:42,972 --> 00:28:45,140
Crank it! Crank it!

256
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:50,562
Look!

257
00:29:10,082 --> 00:29:11,499
MAN: Look out!

258
00:29:23,304 --> 00:29:24,596
(GRUNTS)

259
00:29:34,690 --> 00:29:36,650
Oh, no!

260
00:29:47,745 --> 00:29:49,871
Keep that gas going!
Fill them up!

261
00:30:29,286 --> 00:30:31,287
Helen, come quickly!

262
00:30:31,372 --> 00:30:33,540
Helen! Helen!

263
00:30:44,218 --> 00:30:45,719
(MEN GRUNTING)

264
00:31:02,611 --> 00:31:05,655
You're not supposed to go yet!

265
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:07,782
Infernal machine!

266
00:31:11,620 --> 00:31:14,205
Gregor. Gregor, wait!

267
00:31:14,290 --> 00:31:17,417
Helen, I tried. There was an accident.
I couldn't make it stop. Hurry!

268
00:31:17,543 --> 00:31:19,586
You can make it if you
try. Run! Pease, stop!

269
00:31:19,670 --> 00:31:21,421
Up those stairs.
Run! Quickly!

270
00:31:21,505 --> 00:31:25,383
Wait! Wait! I'll throw
you a rope. Run quickly!

271
00:31:25,509 --> 00:31:28,887
Throw the rope now. Run, Enola.
ENOLA: Slow down! Wait!

272
00:31:28,971 --> 00:31:32,307
No! No!
Wait!

273
00:31:34,810 --> 00:31:36,811
Gregor, don't leave us!
Oh, no!

274
00:31:36,896 --> 00:31:39,147
I'm sorry!
It was an accident!

275
00:31:42,735 --> 00:31:46,988
Helen! Enola!
Helen, I'm sorry!

276
00:31:57,083 --> 00:31:59,334
Shoot him. Shoot him.

277
00:32:26,278 --> 00:32:29,197
If I let you out of here,
you're taking us with you.

278
00:32:30,574 --> 00:32:31,699
Sure.

279
00:32:35,329 --> 00:32:37,288
HELEN: Enola, get down.

280
00:32:38,415 --> 00:32:40,333
Here. Get over here.
Get down.

281
00:32:40,417 --> 00:32:42,168
Hurry! Come on!

282
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:47,048
Get the gate.

283
00:32:49,260 --> 00:32:50,802
(GUNS FIRING)

284
00:33:13,784 --> 00:33:15,285
(GRUNTING)

285
00:33:36,265 --> 00:33:37,640
(GRUNTING)

286
00:33:46,942 --> 00:33:48,359
Hurry up!

287
00:33:48,444 --> 00:33:50,778
Kamikaze! Come on!

288
00:34:19,850 --> 00:34:21,476
That made a hole.

289
00:34:34,156 --> 00:34:36,491
Find her! Get the girl!

290
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:42,872
There!

291
00:34:50,547 --> 00:34:53,800
Go get that guy!
Go! Kill him!

292
00:34:58,389 --> 00:34:59,931
Wow!
Enola!

293
00:35:10,734 --> 00:35:13,820
MARINER: You're too slow!
Run to the other side.

294
00:35:16,407 --> 00:35:17,573
Come on!

295
00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:25,832
Grab on! Hold on, hold on.

296
00:35:30,004 --> 00:35:32,755
It's stuck!
I can't make it through!

297
00:35:32,840 --> 00:35:34,132
Let her go!

298
00:35:36,135 --> 00:35:37,552
Just drop her!

299
00:35:52,484 --> 00:35:55,236
Turn the wheel.
The wheel! Turn it!

300
00:36:00,034 --> 00:36:02,785
That! Come on! Push it!

301
00:36:02,870 --> 00:36:05,997
I am!
Hurry! Now pull!

302
00:36:06,081 --> 00:36:09,834
Push it! Push it hard!
Push it!

303
00:36:09,918 --> 00:36:13,379
Okay, okay! Now pull this!
Pull this! Go! Go!

304
00:36:13,464 --> 00:36:15,882
Pull it! Pull!

305
00:36:28,103 --> 00:36:29,562
Enola, jump.

306
00:36:31,523 --> 00:36:34,150
Jump! Look at me. Jump! Now!
Okay!

307
00:36:50,834 --> 00:36:53,086
Well, excuse me!

308
00:36:53,170 --> 00:36:56,381
Did I say anybody could leave
before the battle was over? Did I?

309
00:36:56,465 --> 00:37:00,009
No! No! Well, do
something. I hate sails.

310
00:37:10,187 --> 00:37:12,688
Hey! Can you steer?

311
00:37:13,107 --> 00:37:14,690
Can I trust you?

312
00:37:30,624 --> 00:37:32,500
(ROPE CREAKING WITH TENSION)

313
00:37:49,518 --> 00:37:52,645
Answer me this, somebody.
Why is that boat still firing?

314
00:37:53,939 --> 00:37:56,566
What's that cousin's name?
Chuck.

315
00:37:56,692 --> 00:38:00,153
Hey, Chuck! He's pulling
him right into us.

316
00:38:00,904 --> 00:38:04,866
Maybe he doesn't answer to
Chuck. Call him Charles. Charles!

317
00:38:06,535 --> 00:38:09,745
Stop him! Stop him!

318
00:38:17,754 --> 00:38:19,213
Charles!

319
00:38:21,717 --> 00:38:23,259
Adios, cousins.

320
00:39:09,348 --> 00:39:11,891
She's here somewhere.
Keep looking.

321
00:39:12,684 --> 00:39:13,935
Stop it!

322
00:39:14,728 --> 00:39:16,604
WOMAN: Let me down!

323
00:39:17,814 --> 00:39:19,440
(PEOPLE CLAMORING)

324
00:39:23,862 --> 00:39:24,904
What you got?

325
00:39:24,988 --> 00:39:27,990
Couple of heartbeats over
there. Aren't saying much.

326
00:39:29,618 --> 00:39:33,579
If you'll notice the arterial nature of
the blood coming from the hole in my head,

327
00:39:33,664 --> 00:39:37,875
you can assume that we're
all having a real lousy day.

328
00:39:37,960 --> 00:39:41,796
So here it is, I need to
know about that tattooed girl.

329
00:39:43,465 --> 00:39:45,883
First one
that tells me lives.

330
00:39:45,968 --> 00:39:50,721
I saw a girl. I saw... It was...
She had a big tattoo on her.

331
00:39:53,475 --> 00:39:55,351
You won. Start over.

332
00:39:55,435 --> 00:39:57,311
I saw a girl.

333
00:39:57,396 --> 00:40:02,024
I'm not sure, but I think she
got on that boat with three hulls.

334
00:40:03,151 --> 00:40:05,570
The boat that cost me
1,000 G's of go-juice?

335
00:40:05,654 --> 00:40:07,572
Yeah, the mute-o's boat.

336
00:40:07,656 --> 00:40:11,576
Mute-o?
Yeah. He got these slits here.

337
00:40:11,660 --> 00:40:15,162
Like fish gills.

338
00:40:15,247 --> 00:40:17,248
He wasn't really a man.

339
00:40:17,332 --> 00:40:20,626
A fluke of evolution.

340
00:40:21,670 --> 00:40:24,005
Oh, I'm sorry. A fluke of...

341
00:40:24,089 --> 00:40:26,465
Evolution.
I know!

342
00:40:26,550 --> 00:40:30,595
No! No, you said you wouldn't
kill me! Did I say that?

343
00:40:30,679 --> 00:40:33,598
Witnesses, anybody, did I?
Yes, yes, yes! You said...

344
00:40:33,682 --> 00:40:35,683
You said it.
Oh, I may have.

345
00:40:38,103 --> 00:40:39,562
I may have.

346
00:40:41,732 --> 00:40:43,065
(SCREAMING)

347
00:40:46,778 --> 00:40:48,654
We get to the 'Deez,

348
00:40:50,282 --> 00:40:53,284
tank up that sky boat,
send it out on patrol.

349
00:40:54,703 --> 00:40:59,707
We gotta keep an eye out
for that ichthy-freak.

350
00:41:23,774 --> 00:41:25,608
You been there, haven't you?

351
00:41:30,530 --> 00:41:31,906
Dryland?

352
00:41:33,241 --> 00:41:34,742
You know where it is.

353
00:41:36,370 --> 00:41:38,204
Yeah, I know where it is.

354
00:41:41,291 --> 00:41:44,251
And... And we're going?

355
00:41:45,087 --> 00:41:46,754
You and I are.

356
00:41:48,757 --> 00:41:51,050
The kid we gotta pitch
over the side.

357
00:41:53,929 --> 00:41:56,764
What?
My boat's tore up.

358
00:41:57,933 --> 00:42:00,434
I'm taking on water.

359
00:42:00,519 --> 00:42:03,604
I'd be lucky to get half
a hydro ration out of that.

360
00:42:03,689 --> 00:42:07,149
You know...
I said I won't drink.

361
00:42:07,859 --> 00:42:09,110
For 12 days?

362
00:42:11,822 --> 00:42:16,701
No. It's better one of you dies
now than both of you die slow.

363
00:42:16,785 --> 00:42:18,035
Wait. Wait.

364
00:42:19,162 --> 00:42:22,206
We saved your life.
We got you out.

365
00:42:22,290 --> 00:42:25,793
No, you got me out so you
could get out. We're even.

366
00:42:25,877 --> 00:42:27,920
She can cook. She can fish.

367
00:42:28,046 --> 00:42:30,297
So can I. Then take my
necklace. Take my necklace.

368
00:42:30,382 --> 00:42:32,633
I got better ones below.
No, look!

369
00:42:35,971 --> 00:42:38,723
After what you went through
back there on the atoll,

370
00:42:38,807 --> 00:42:41,142
I can understand
why you would want to.

371
00:42:41,935 --> 00:42:43,853
But she's a child.

372
00:42:46,440 --> 00:42:48,023
(HUMMING)

373
00:42:55,073 --> 00:42:56,991
Is there something else, then?

374
00:43:02,831 --> 00:43:04,665
Enola! Go below.

375
00:43:06,418 --> 00:43:07,626
Enola.

376
00:43:18,764 --> 00:43:19,930
Yeah.

377
00:43:20,682 --> 00:43:22,516
You said so yourself.

378
00:43:24,019 --> 00:43:25,853
Been out there a long time.

379
00:44:12,400 --> 00:44:15,319
Couple hours ago,
I was potential dirt to you.

380
00:44:15,654 --> 00:44:17,279
We're not like that.

381
00:44:17,906 --> 00:44:19,240
You all are.

382
00:44:24,871 --> 00:44:27,164
Know what I was
really thinking?

383
00:44:27,791 --> 00:44:30,918
Why I don't drop both of you
in my wake right now.

384
00:44:32,379 --> 00:44:34,129
You got nothing I need.

385
00:44:50,772 --> 00:44:52,940
You're taking us to Dryland.

386
00:44:57,821 --> 00:45:00,614
Killing's a hard thing
to do well.

387
00:45:00,699 --> 00:45:04,201
Believe me. I'm not the
one to start on. Both of us!

388
00:45:09,624 --> 00:45:12,001
How long you plan
to hold that on me?

389
00:45:12,085 --> 00:45:13,878
As long as it takes.

390
00:45:17,132 --> 00:45:19,466
Okay.
No!

391
00:45:21,303 --> 00:45:24,388
HELEN: Let me out of here!

392
00:45:24,472 --> 00:45:28,309
Where the hell are you?
Don't you touch my child.

393
00:45:28,393 --> 00:45:30,227
Come here!

394
00:45:31,563 --> 00:45:33,314
(HELEN GRUNTING)

395
00:46:18,610 --> 00:46:21,445
Okay, now,
don't move, okay? There.

396
00:46:21,529 --> 00:46:24,782
Oh, looking real good, yeah.

397
00:46:24,866 --> 00:46:27,201
There. There.

398
00:46:27,285 --> 00:46:29,078
All done.

399
00:46:29,162 --> 00:46:32,665
Now, there may be some small
problem in depth perception.

400
00:46:32,749 --> 00:46:35,417
Well, it better not screw up
my short game.

401
00:46:37,587 --> 00:46:38,587
Well?

402
00:46:38,672 --> 00:46:40,297
Looks good.
I like it. Yeah.

403
00:46:40,382 --> 00:46:43,258
I like it better than
your real eye. Much better.

404
00:46:43,385 --> 00:46:47,221
What do you say, Toby?
The truth. Looks like shit.

405
00:46:47,305 --> 00:46:50,057
That's why I love children.
No guile.

406
00:46:54,229 --> 00:46:56,438
It does look like shit.

407
00:46:57,482 --> 00:46:59,775
And it feels like cold shit!

408
00:46:59,859 --> 00:47:02,569
Deacon?
What...

409
00:47:03,530 --> 00:47:07,157
I'm sorry. It's just that
there's a problem in the pit.

410
00:47:07,242 --> 00:47:08,867
Maybe you should come.

411
00:47:14,457 --> 00:47:15,791
Let's drive.

412
00:47:24,092 --> 00:47:26,927
The pit. Don't bother
with the scenic route.

413
00:47:27,012 --> 00:47:28,345
Drive!

414
00:47:30,098 --> 00:47:31,807
Hey, it's driving better.

415
00:47:44,696 --> 00:47:46,113
Get off!

416
00:47:47,615 --> 00:47:50,242
Get off.
Get out of the way.

417
00:47:50,326 --> 00:47:52,911
All right, all right,
all right. Okay, okay.

418
00:47:55,457 --> 00:47:59,043
Bless you, cousins. Bless you.
Bless all you cousins over there.

419
00:47:59,127 --> 00:48:00,419
Keep up the good work.

420
00:48:00,503 --> 00:48:03,464
Growth is progress.
Growth is progress.

421
00:48:03,548 --> 00:48:08,218
DEPTH GAUGE GUY:
Somebody! Hey, up there!

422
00:48:08,303 --> 00:48:10,220
Take it off!

423
00:48:13,141 --> 00:48:15,934
Yes.
Your Deaconship.

424
00:48:16,019 --> 00:48:18,729
Hello. Good morning.

425
00:48:18,813 --> 00:48:22,441
Or night, whichever
the case may be.

426
00:48:22,525 --> 00:48:26,320
What is it? I'm a busy man.
I thought you should know.

427
00:48:26,738 --> 00:48:31,283
We're down to exactly
four feet, nine inches

428
00:48:31,367 --> 00:48:33,118
of black stuff.

429
00:48:37,165 --> 00:48:38,665
Thank you, sir.

430
00:48:38,708 --> 00:48:42,086
How many G's is that after
refining? Maybe three refuelers.

431
00:48:42,212 --> 00:48:44,797
We'll burn through that
in two lunars. Sweet Joe!

432
00:48:44,881 --> 00:48:48,175
Will somebody please tell me
what's happening to this place?

433
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:50,511
We outgrowed it.

434
00:48:51,429 --> 00:48:55,432
All right, the only thing that
is important is the tattooed girl.

435
00:48:55,517 --> 00:48:58,435
We don't spare any go-juice
finding her.

436
00:48:58,520 --> 00:49:02,106
We don't waste it anywhere else.
You cancel all those tractor pulls

437
00:49:02,190 --> 00:49:04,024
and all that stuff
until later.

438
00:49:04,109 --> 00:49:05,442
You got it?

439
00:49:07,195 --> 00:49:10,155
Dryland is the mother lode.

440
00:49:11,366 --> 00:49:12,741
On your toes.

441
00:49:14,327 --> 00:49:15,577
Excellent.

442
00:49:45,608 --> 00:49:47,734
Hi.
Move.

443
00:49:48,486 --> 00:49:50,904
HELEN: Enola! Enola, come here!

444
00:49:53,032 --> 00:49:54,283
Come on!

445
00:49:57,078 --> 00:49:59,204
Enola, come over here.

446
00:50:12,969 --> 00:50:14,261
Hey!

447
00:50:17,098 --> 00:50:21,560
What are you doing? Decorating
your boat. It's ugly.

448
00:50:25,273 --> 00:50:28,025
Where did you get this?
From down below.

449
00:50:28,109 --> 00:50:31,153
This is mine! You don't
touch anything of mine.

450
00:50:31,237 --> 00:50:32,362
I drew it for you.

451
00:50:32,447 --> 00:50:34,948
You don't draw on anything.
You understand?

452
00:50:40,205 --> 00:50:41,622
MARINER: What...

453
00:50:44,417 --> 00:50:47,461
This is my boat.
I got it the way I like it.

454
00:50:47,545 --> 00:50:50,214
You take up space
and you slow me down.

455
00:50:53,968 --> 00:50:56,762
You know,
she's just a little girl.

456
00:50:56,888 --> 00:51:00,015
She doesn't know your
rules. You wanna stay?

457
00:51:00,099 --> 00:51:01,642
You teach her.

458
00:51:21,412 --> 00:51:23,455
HELEN: Enola, what'd I tell you?

459
00:51:28,920 --> 00:51:31,088
You're not so tough.
You know that?

460
00:51:35,635 --> 00:51:37,678
How many people
have you killed?

461
00:51:40,473 --> 00:51:43,350
Ten? Twenty?

462
00:51:45,228 --> 00:51:49,189
You talk a lot. I talk a lot
'cause you don't talk at all.

463
00:51:49,899 --> 00:51:51,608
Now, how many?

464
00:51:53,695 --> 00:51:55,028
Including little girls?

465
00:51:55,947 --> 00:51:57,406
I'm not afraid of you.

466
00:52:01,077 --> 00:52:04,538
I told Helen you wouldn't be
so ugly if you cut your hair.

467
00:52:08,501 --> 00:52:10,252
In fact,
you talk all the time.

468
00:52:10,378 --> 00:52:12,713
It's like a storm when you're around.
HELEN: Enola!

469
00:52:12,797 --> 00:52:14,506
Hey! What are you doing?

470
00:52:15,383 --> 00:52:18,760
Helen! You bastard! She can't swim!

471
00:52:19,178 --> 00:52:22,055
ENOLA: Help me! Helen!

472
00:52:23,349 --> 00:52:25,309
Helen, help me!

473
00:52:25,893 --> 00:52:27,311
Helen!

474
00:52:27,979 --> 00:52:30,063
Helen, help me!

475
00:52:32,317 --> 00:52:33,775
(GASPING)

476
00:52:38,406 --> 00:52:41,158
Helen! Helen!

477
00:52:41,242 --> 00:52:42,868
Hold on!

478
00:52:43,786 --> 00:52:45,954
Look at me. Look at me.
Look at me.

479
00:52:47,373 --> 00:52:51,293
(GASPING) Oh, please, come back. Please!

480
00:52:51,377 --> 00:52:54,504
Please! Oh, please!

481
00:52:54,964 --> 00:52:56,506
(HELEN COUGHING VIOLENTLY)

482
00:52:59,594 --> 00:53:00,761
HELEN: Hey!

483
00:53:03,890 --> 00:53:06,641
Hold on. Okay, almost.

484
00:53:19,989 --> 00:53:20,739
Thanks. I swear,
if you ever touch her again...

485
00:53:20,740 --> 00:53:24,993
Thanks. I swear,
if you ever touch her again...

486
00:53:25,078 --> 00:53:27,120
You son of a bitch.
Look at me.

487
00:53:27,205 --> 00:53:29,915
You will go to bed that night
and you will never wake up.

488
00:53:29,999 --> 00:53:32,292
Shut up.
What?

489
00:53:33,086 --> 00:53:35,337
Smokers?
Yeah.

490
00:53:35,421 --> 00:53:37,672
Can't we outrun them?

491
00:53:39,967 --> 00:53:41,593
Not with my sail down.

492
00:53:59,654 --> 00:54:01,196
What are you thinking about?

493
00:54:01,531 --> 00:54:02,989
That's a nice touch.

494
00:54:03,825 --> 00:54:05,158
That's them.

495
00:54:12,083 --> 00:54:13,834
Don't hit the kid.

496
00:54:19,298 --> 00:54:20,549
(LAUGHING DEVILISHLY)

497
00:54:29,684 --> 00:54:30,809
HELEN: Hey!

498
00:54:37,024 --> 00:54:38,483
Coward!

499
00:54:47,452 --> 00:54:48,618
Fuck!

500
00:55:17,231 --> 00:55:18,231
No!

501
00:55:23,070 --> 00:55:24,696
(GROANING)

502
00:55:31,996 --> 00:55:32,996
Joe!

503
00:55:40,421 --> 00:55:41,630
Cut it!

504
00:55:50,848 --> 00:55:52,974
Come on! Come on!

505
00:56:26,884 --> 00:56:29,219
All right. Okay.

506
00:57:29,697 --> 00:57:32,198
HELEN: Oh, I'm really sorry.

507
00:57:32,283 --> 00:57:35,660
I'm sorry. But you didn't
give me much choice.

508
00:57:35,745 --> 00:57:37,078
Oh, I know.

509
00:57:37,163 --> 00:57:40,874
But you ran away. I... What?

510
00:57:40,958 --> 00:57:44,252
What are you...
No! Please, no! No!

511
00:57:49,550 --> 00:57:51,217
(GRUNTING)

512
00:57:54,055 --> 00:57:58,516
Don't ever touch anything
on my boat again.

513
00:58:01,979 --> 00:58:03,897
She said she was sorry!

514
00:58:08,235 --> 00:58:11,029
That means you're supposed
to say something back!

515
00:58:33,552 --> 00:58:35,387
Did you say something?
ENOLA: Mmm-mmm.

516
00:58:59,412 --> 00:59:01,162
DEACON: The child...
Did he have the girl with him?

517
00:59:01,247 --> 00:59:02,330
NORD: Uh-huh.

518
00:59:02,415 --> 00:59:06,876
Seems to me if we launch now, we
can cut him down just about here.

519
00:59:07,962 --> 00:59:11,214
If he doesn't change course.
It's not likely.

520
00:59:11,298 --> 00:59:14,884
He's a wily one,
that ichthy-demon.

521
00:59:15,302 --> 00:59:17,679
Yeah, if he knows
he's been spotted,

522
00:59:17,763 --> 00:59:21,599
he'll expect us to expect
him to change course,

523
00:59:21,684 --> 00:59:25,186
which is exactly
why he'll keep his heading.

524
00:59:25,271 --> 00:59:28,481
The important question is,
where's he heading?

525
00:59:28,566 --> 00:59:32,152
And does he know
how precious his cargo is?

526
00:59:32,570 --> 00:59:36,448
I say that he's headed here,
and we take him here.

527
00:59:44,331 --> 00:59:45,999
DRIFTER: Hello!

528
00:59:47,835 --> 00:59:49,127
Drifter?

529
00:59:49,211 --> 00:59:50,420
Mmm-hmm.

530
00:59:50,504 --> 00:59:52,172
Are we gonna stop?

531
00:59:52,256 --> 00:59:54,174
Look at the flag!
His flag's up.

532
00:59:54,258 --> 00:59:56,718
I thought you all
stopped for each other.

533
00:59:57,219 --> 00:59:58,845
(DRIFTER YELLING)

534
01:00:01,223 --> 01:00:04,642
Well, maybe
he has some food!

535
01:00:07,354 --> 01:00:09,731
Don't find many who follow
the rules anymore.

536
01:00:09,815 --> 01:00:13,318
Well, make it quick.
What's your business?

537
01:00:18,491 --> 01:00:19,616
Trade.

538
01:00:22,328 --> 01:00:23,703
You got any resin?

539
01:00:23,954 --> 01:00:25,455
(CHUCKLING)

540
01:00:27,208 --> 01:00:31,377
Resin? He's gotta be out of his mind. He's
out of his mind. I don't have any resin.

541
01:00:31,796 --> 01:00:34,339
You been outwater a long time,
haven't you?

542
01:00:35,341 --> 01:00:38,176
Must be joking me, man. Resin!
You don't got enough.

543
01:00:38,260 --> 01:00:39,969
How about food?

544
01:00:40,054 --> 01:00:42,013
Could you say that again,
please?

545
01:00:42,097 --> 01:00:43,973
How about food?

546
01:00:44,058 --> 01:00:47,519
Yeah, food.
Yeah, that'd be good, eh?

547
01:00:47,603 --> 01:00:50,563
I haven't eaten in,
well, you know, forever.

548
01:00:52,566 --> 01:00:56,402
Which one of you two is the cook?
'Cause I usually fall for the waitress.

549
01:00:56,612 --> 01:00:57,612
(SNICKERS)

550
01:00:58,447 --> 01:01:00,240
Like you said,
I don't have much.

551
01:01:00,324 --> 01:01:03,243
I'll say. Let's see.

552
01:01:03,702 --> 01:01:06,746
We can start with that wee
orchard you got over there.

553
01:01:07,373 --> 01:01:08,790
(EXCLAIMING)

554
01:01:09,625 --> 01:01:11,417
MARINER: All right, hold it there.

555
01:01:12,419 --> 01:01:14,420
We can talk.
Fair enough.

556
01:01:16,841 --> 01:01:18,341
(MUTTERING)

557
01:01:20,803 --> 01:01:23,054
It's a nice rod, isn't it?

558
01:01:23,138 --> 01:01:25,348
I like that rod.
It's a nice rod.

559
01:01:32,606 --> 01:01:35,358
Got yourself a wee harem
going here, now, don't you, eh?

560
01:01:41,782 --> 01:01:43,741
What do you want
for the women?

561
01:01:43,826 --> 01:01:45,493
We're not for sale.

562
01:01:46,662 --> 01:01:49,372
Not for sale? There's no
such thing as not for sale.

563
01:01:49,456 --> 01:01:51,916
"Not for sale"!
Not for sale, eh?

564
01:01:53,127 --> 01:01:56,880
Are they a pair, or would you
consider selling them separate?

565
01:01:56,964 --> 01:01:58,882
No. Our business
is done here

566
01:01:58,966 --> 01:02:01,134
unless you got some resin
for sale you can part with.

567
01:02:01,218 --> 01:02:03,219
I told you once already,
I don't have it.

568
01:02:03,304 --> 01:02:05,722
I don't have it.
Don't have it. Don't have it.

569
01:02:05,806 --> 01:02:09,976
Haven't seen that there for trade in
lunars now. Wait. Wait, wait, wait.

570
01:02:13,314 --> 01:02:16,357
I do have something that'll
make you change your mind.

571
01:02:17,192 --> 01:02:19,485
Something that
you can't pass on.

572
01:02:19,820 --> 01:02:21,237
(SNICKERING)

573
01:02:21,614 --> 01:02:24,198
I took it off
an atoller refugee camp.

574
01:02:25,159 --> 01:02:27,493
The life savings
of the entire clan.

575
01:02:35,336 --> 01:02:38,338
Paper. Look at my wee paper.

576
01:02:38,714 --> 01:02:43,468
It's paper. Have you ever
seen paper? Look at it.

577
01:02:43,552 --> 01:02:44,844
Smell it.

578
01:02:44,929 --> 01:02:45,929
(SNIFFING)

579
01:02:47,306 --> 01:02:49,557
I been saving it
for a special trade.

580
01:02:52,561 --> 01:02:54,103
Don't you do it.

581
01:02:54,855 --> 01:02:57,774
You know, maybe I'm talking
to the wrong person here.

582
01:02:57,858 --> 01:03:00,652
Whose boat is this?
Is it your boat or your boat?

583
01:03:00,736 --> 01:03:03,821
No. No, it's his boat.
But he doesn't own us.

584
01:03:11,205 --> 01:03:12,538
Half an hour.

585
01:03:14,208 --> 01:03:16,668
Is he out of his mind
or something here?

586
01:03:16,752 --> 01:03:18,628
I got two pages in here, man!

587
01:03:18,712 --> 01:03:20,797
I mean, I'd get half a dozen
girls with this, you know.

588
01:03:20,881 --> 01:03:22,048
Half an hour.

589
01:03:23,717 --> 01:03:24,968
You trading or not?

590
01:03:25,052 --> 01:03:26,052
Don't.

591
01:03:26,136 --> 01:03:27,220
Shut up.

592
01:03:29,181 --> 01:03:30,264
Okay.

593
01:03:32,893 --> 01:03:35,395
Forty-five minutes
with the wee one over there.

594
01:03:35,479 --> 01:03:37,939
I like to do the talking,
if you know what I mean.

595
01:03:39,149 --> 01:03:40,316
No!

596
01:03:42,444 --> 01:03:45,780
Fair enough.
Come, come, come, come, come!

597
01:03:46,365 --> 01:03:48,408
No. My boat.

598
01:03:50,285 --> 01:03:52,704
Fair enough. Fair enough.
Good, good, good. Aye.

599
01:03:52,788 --> 01:03:53,997
Helen.

600
01:03:54,081 --> 01:03:57,417
Sit down.
Enola, just do as he says.

601
01:03:59,670 --> 01:04:01,045
(SNICKERING)

602
01:04:43,297 --> 01:04:45,214
It's been a while
for me now.

603
01:04:46,383 --> 01:04:49,427
Guess it's been
more than a while, actually.

604
01:04:52,890 --> 01:04:57,810
I've only got half an hour. Half an
hour, half an hour, half an hour...

605
01:04:59,229 --> 01:05:01,314
Right, right, right...

606
01:05:02,816 --> 01:05:05,902
You're so beautiful. Aye!
Anyone ever told you that?

607
01:05:05,986 --> 01:05:09,155
Give me that. What are you
doing? It's just a game.

608
01:05:12,326 --> 01:05:15,495
Oh, I... I...
We was just playing.

609
01:05:18,582 --> 01:05:20,625
The trade's off.

610
01:05:21,168 --> 01:05:23,044
I changed my mind.

611
01:05:23,462 --> 01:05:26,714
Well, you can't do that.

612
01:05:26,799 --> 01:05:29,175
I mean, a deal's a deal, aye?

613
01:05:29,843 --> 01:05:31,761
I just did.

614
01:05:31,970 --> 01:05:33,471
Hey!

615
01:05:33,931 --> 01:05:38,059
She's mine. Aye. No.
No, no, I won't have it, no.

616
01:05:38,143 --> 01:05:40,978
Go on. Get, get, get.
Go, go, go. Good, good.

617
01:05:41,063 --> 01:05:45,399
But don't go too far, now.
This won't take but a minute.

618
01:05:50,239 --> 01:05:54,951
I hate this boat.
I hate this boat!

619
01:05:55,953 --> 01:05:57,745
You bastard.

620
01:06:02,876 --> 01:06:07,880
You're pretty stingy for a man who's
got everything, aren't you, now?

621
01:06:22,146 --> 01:06:23,729
(CLANGING)

622
01:06:24,398 --> 01:06:26,399
DRIFTER: You bastard!

623
01:06:37,911 --> 01:06:39,579
Where's me boat?

624
01:06:41,039 --> 01:06:42,415
Where's me boat?

625
01:06:50,340 --> 01:06:51,799
Help me to me boat.

626
01:06:55,012 --> 01:06:56,179
(GROANING) Me boat.

627
01:07:29,338 --> 01:07:30,922
HELEN: What the... No!

628
01:07:32,799 --> 01:07:35,593
Never catch anything
with this. It's useless.

629
01:07:41,683 --> 01:07:46,395
HELEN: Look, I'm sorry.
You were right about the pole.

630
01:07:47,314 --> 01:07:49,982
You were right,
but we have to eat.

631
01:07:55,239 --> 01:07:57,240
Now, I know you can hear me!

632
01:07:57,324 --> 01:08:00,826
Now, we can fish for ourselves.
You show us what to do.

633
01:08:00,911 --> 01:08:03,537
You must know how.
Even you gotta eat!

634
01:08:03,622 --> 01:08:05,081
You just sit there.

635
01:08:29,189 --> 01:08:30,564
What...

636
01:08:30,649 --> 01:08:32,024
I don't know.

637
01:08:51,044 --> 01:08:52,253
(SEA MONSTER ROARING)

638
01:09:01,680 --> 01:09:03,055
(GUN FIRING)

639
01:09:08,353 --> 01:09:09,937
Happy now?

640
01:09:14,401 --> 01:09:15,860
(HUMMING)

641
01:09:28,373 --> 01:09:30,499
You don't like my singing,
do you?

642
01:09:33,837 --> 01:09:37,256
Helen says you don't like my
singing 'cause you can't sing.

643
01:09:43,597 --> 01:09:45,890
Ever try and listen?
To what?

644
01:09:48,727 --> 01:09:50,603
The sound of the world.

645
01:09:55,400 --> 01:09:58,486
I don't hear anything.
That's because you're too loud

646
01:09:58,570 --> 01:10:00,529
and you're moving around
all the time.

647
01:10:00,614 --> 01:10:02,281
Try sitting still.

648
01:10:04,242 --> 01:10:05,701
Eyeball?

649
01:10:21,468 --> 01:10:23,636
I wish I had feet like his.

650
01:10:25,889 --> 01:10:27,681
Then maybe I could swim.

651
01:10:27,766 --> 01:10:29,225
Enola.

652
01:10:32,854 --> 01:10:33,854
(BURPS)

653
01:10:34,356 --> 01:10:35,648
Sorry.

654
01:10:40,112 --> 01:10:42,571
Never met a person
who couldn't swim before.

655
01:10:45,200 --> 01:10:46,492
I can't.

656
01:10:49,037 --> 01:10:50,955
People said I was weird.

657
01:10:51,832 --> 01:10:53,749
Maybe they were right.

658
01:10:57,087 --> 01:10:59,338
Maybe they were right
about you, too.

659
01:11:27,367 --> 01:11:29,326
Enola wanted
to give you this.

660
01:11:33,832 --> 01:11:35,332
And this.

661
01:11:38,712 --> 01:11:40,421
She won't take it again.

662
01:11:46,887 --> 01:11:48,804
You don't look
much like her.

663
01:11:50,056 --> 01:11:52,725
Well, I'm not her mother,
if that's what you're asking.

664
01:11:52,809 --> 01:11:54,185
You act like it.

665
01:11:57,731 --> 01:11:59,899
What are those marks
on her back?

666
01:11:59,983 --> 01:12:02,985
Nothing.
They're just fanciful things.

667
01:12:04,112 --> 01:12:05,613
Like what she draws?

668
01:12:05,697 --> 01:12:07,406
She draws what she sees.

669
01:12:08,200 --> 01:12:09,658
She's like a mirror.

670
01:12:11,077 --> 01:12:13,496
People on the atoll
thought she was a...

671
01:12:15,248 --> 01:12:16,373
Freak?

672
01:12:19,085 --> 01:12:20,252
Yeah.

673
01:12:20,337 --> 01:12:22,338
I just thought
she was special.

674
01:12:30,722 --> 01:12:34,016
I'm not giving it to her.
It's just...

675
01:12:34,100 --> 01:12:35,392
A loan.

676
01:12:36,436 --> 01:12:39,271
Yeah. I'm not
giving it to her.

677
01:12:44,694 --> 01:12:46,320
Is Dryland beautiful?

678
01:12:49,115 --> 01:12:50,366
You'll see.

679
01:13:10,053 --> 01:13:11,679
(ENOLA GIGGLING)

680
01:13:17,143 --> 01:13:18,310
Enola?

681
01:13:19,521 --> 01:13:21,897
Enola, what are you doing?

682
01:13:21,982 --> 01:13:23,732
Those monsters'll kill her!

683
01:13:23,817 --> 01:13:25,484
No, they're asleep now.

684
01:13:28,530 --> 01:13:30,072
Hands around my neck.

685
01:13:32,158 --> 01:13:34,994
Let the water tell your arms
and legs how to move.

686
01:13:35,495 --> 01:13:37,788
We're going down.
Hold your breath.

687
01:15:16,304 --> 01:15:19,973
What is it?
What is it?

688
01:15:21,601 --> 01:15:23,727
Barter outpost.

689
01:15:24,104 --> 01:15:26,271
You said we'd get
to Dryland today.

690
01:15:26,648 --> 01:15:29,525
Today. Tomorrow.
What's it matter?

691
01:15:30,527 --> 01:15:32,027
I need resin.

692
01:15:34,280 --> 01:15:35,823
(SPEAKING PORTUGREEK)

693
01:15:42,414 --> 01:15:43,664
What is that?

694
01:15:43,748 --> 01:15:45,124
It's PortuGreek.

695
01:15:46,668 --> 01:15:48,168
Their own language.

696
01:15:50,505 --> 01:15:52,548
Or so I thought.

697
01:15:58,471 --> 01:15:59,680
(SNIFFING)

698
01:16:21,870 --> 01:16:25,122
There's my girl. Yeah.

699
01:16:25,206 --> 01:16:29,084
Howdy, darling.
Come on in here now.

700
01:16:58,072 --> 01:17:00,991
What's going on?
Smokers.

701
01:17:04,120 --> 01:17:05,746
What the hell?

702
01:17:09,542 --> 01:17:12,628
He's spotted us. Sound it!

703
01:17:12,712 --> 01:17:16,632
Sound it! Sound it!
Sound it! Sound it!

704
01:17:28,394 --> 01:17:29,895
(WHOOPING)

705
01:17:37,904 --> 01:17:39,196
HELEN: Enola!

706
01:17:52,293 --> 01:17:54,294
Starboard side! Now!

707
01:17:55,588 --> 01:17:57,005
Get in the chair!

708
01:18:03,721 --> 01:18:05,764
Watch it.
He's clearing the net.

709
01:18:18,069 --> 01:18:19,403
Go to the helm.

710
01:18:22,532 --> 01:18:24,575
Get the centerboard!
Centerboard?

711
01:18:24,659 --> 01:18:27,452
What centerboard?
I don't know what to do!

712
01:18:29,914 --> 01:18:31,957
It's too late. Over here!

713
01:18:45,805 --> 01:18:47,389
(SCREAMING)

714
01:18:51,477 --> 01:18:53,103
No!

715
01:19:09,871 --> 01:19:11,413
Give me that rifle.

716
01:19:12,332 --> 01:19:17,002
Boy, if you wanna do something
right, you've got to do it yourself.

717
01:19:17,420 --> 01:19:18,879
All right.

718
01:19:20,506 --> 01:19:21,506
(GRUNTS)

719
01:19:26,054 --> 01:19:27,346
I winged him.

720
01:19:27,430 --> 01:19:30,933
I say we load all the go-juice onto
one boat and run the freak down.

721
01:19:31,017 --> 01:19:34,603
You can't catch him in ten boats,
and you wanna send just one?

722
01:19:34,687 --> 01:19:37,272
Well, I pray that
you're joking.

723
01:19:45,531 --> 01:19:47,282
Bring me the trackers.

724
01:19:53,748 --> 01:19:55,165
He's hurt.

725
01:19:56,084 --> 01:19:58,585
HELEN: It's not what you
think. They weren't after her.

726
01:19:58,670 --> 01:20:00,087
I saw what I saw.

727
01:20:00,171 --> 01:20:01,296
What?

728
01:20:05,468 --> 01:20:07,010
No more lies.

729
01:20:08,513 --> 01:20:09,513
(SHRIEKING)

730
01:20:09,597 --> 01:20:11,682
What are the marks
on her back?

731
01:20:12,100 --> 01:20:15,310
People say
it's the way to Dryland.

732
01:20:15,395 --> 01:20:17,062
Dryland's a myth!

733
01:20:17,146 --> 01:20:20,065
No! You said so yourself
that you know where it is.

734
01:20:20,733 --> 01:20:21,900
You did.

735
01:20:27,031 --> 01:20:30,409
You're a fool to believe in
something you've never seen before.

736
01:20:30,493 --> 01:20:34,663
I've seen it. I've touched it! Dirt
that was richer and darker than yours.

737
01:20:34,747 --> 01:20:36,707
It was in the basket
we found Enola in!

738
01:20:36,791 --> 01:20:39,376
It doesn't exist! Well,
how can you be so sure?

739
01:20:39,460 --> 01:20:44,297
Because I've sailed farther than most
have dreamed, and I've never seen it.

740
01:20:45,925 --> 01:20:47,926
But the things on your boat!

741
01:20:48,845 --> 01:20:51,263
"Things on my boat" what?

742
01:20:51,347 --> 01:20:54,766
There are things on your boat
that nobody has ever seen!

743
01:20:54,851 --> 01:20:57,060
What are these shells?

744
01:20:57,145 --> 01:21:00,105
And that reflecting glass?

745
01:21:00,189 --> 01:21:03,483
(STUTTERING)
And the music box? That...

746
01:21:04,777 --> 01:21:08,196
Well, if not from Dryland,
then where? Where?

747
01:21:11,242 --> 01:21:12,909
You wanna see Dryland?

748
01:21:14,829 --> 01:21:16,788
You really wanna see it?

749
01:21:18,499 --> 01:21:20,584
I'll take you to Dryland.

750
01:21:28,426 --> 01:21:29,801
Get in.

751
01:21:36,017 --> 01:21:39,227
Well, what about Enola?
There's only air for one.

752
01:21:39,312 --> 01:21:41,605
Get in the water.
It'll be all right.

753
01:21:47,278 --> 01:21:48,987
Don't touch anything.

754
01:21:53,993 --> 01:21:55,452
Okay?

755
01:25:26,789 --> 01:25:28,248
I didn't know.

756
01:25:30,418 --> 01:25:32,210
All this time...

757
01:25:33,754 --> 01:25:35,588
I didn't know.

758
01:25:35,673 --> 01:25:37,215
Nobody does.

759
01:25:45,558 --> 01:25:47,517
Can you get us out of here?

760
01:25:47,601 --> 01:25:51,104
DEACON: I'd say there're
two chances of that.

761
01:25:51,188 --> 01:25:53,606
Hey!
No way and no how.

762
01:25:54,400 --> 01:25:57,402
Should've bought me
that drink. Come on!

763
01:25:57,486 --> 01:25:59,070
This way. Come on!

764
01:26:00,740 --> 01:26:02,407
(SMOKERS WHOOPING)

765
01:26:06,412 --> 01:26:08,580
DEACON:
Proper introductions first.

766
01:26:09,415 --> 01:26:10,915
I'm the Deacon.

767
01:26:16,213 --> 01:26:20,842
If you don't recall the face, perhaps
it's 'cause I didn't always look like this.

768
01:26:24,221 --> 01:26:28,558
Now, I suspect
she's somewhere real close.

769
01:26:28,642 --> 01:26:30,935
So we can tear this boat apart
looking for her,

770
01:26:31,020 --> 01:26:33,688
but I'd rather
somebody tell me where she is.

771
01:26:33,773 --> 01:26:36,483
And the first one
that does that lives.

772
01:26:36,567 --> 01:26:41,404
And the runner-up... Well,
actually, there are no runners-up.

773
01:26:43,908 --> 01:26:46,284
Oh, sweet Joe,
I love this part.

774
01:26:46,369 --> 01:26:49,454
Choose them.
One, two. Live or die.

775
01:26:50,664 --> 01:26:53,833
You know, personally,

776
01:26:53,959 --> 01:26:56,920
I'd rather shoot
the sperm-of-the-devil here.

777
01:26:58,464 --> 01:27:00,256
But you know something?

778
01:27:00,341 --> 01:27:02,842
I don't think you're
gonna tell me, are you?

779
01:27:02,927 --> 01:27:04,969
Too bad.

780
01:27:05,971 --> 01:27:09,349
Come on. What do you say?

781
01:27:09,433 --> 01:27:11,476
She's not your kind.

782
01:27:12,978 --> 01:27:15,271
You don't even
have a kind.

783
01:27:15,356 --> 01:27:19,109
If you say it,
he'll still kill us both.

784
01:27:19,819 --> 01:27:21,444
(DEACON GRUNTING)

785
01:27:23,864 --> 01:27:25,990
Let's not get ahead
of ourselves.

786
01:27:27,827 --> 01:27:29,744
If you don't tell me,

787
01:27:29,829 --> 01:27:32,831
I swear to Poseidon
I will torch your boat.

788
01:27:39,839 --> 01:27:42,590
DEACON: Refresh my memory. What
happens when neither of them talks?

789
01:27:42,675 --> 01:27:45,510
Never happened before.
Never happened before.

790
01:27:46,887 --> 01:27:48,221
All right!

791
01:27:48,305 --> 01:27:50,932
If they won't tell us
where she is, do them!

792
01:27:51,016 --> 01:27:53,393
Both of them! Kill them now!

793
01:27:53,477 --> 01:27:54,811
No!

794
01:27:55,229 --> 01:27:56,229
HELEN: No!

795
01:27:56,313 --> 01:27:58,857
So gullible! Bring her here.

796
01:28:00,860 --> 01:28:03,695
Take and ye shall receive!
HELEN: No! Leave her!

797
01:28:03,779 --> 01:28:06,698
So ends your daily sermonette!

798
01:28:06,782 --> 01:28:10,201
All we gotta do is figure out
this map. Turn her upside down.

799
01:28:10,327 --> 01:28:13,788
Does that mean anything to you? No.

800
01:28:13,873 --> 01:28:17,208
We'll figure it out back at the
'Deez. Back at the 'Deez, boys.

801
01:28:17,293 --> 01:28:19,627
Careful with her.
Careful with her!

802
01:28:19,712 --> 01:28:22,130
What about them?
Do them both.

803
01:28:22,548 --> 01:28:23,798
Torch the boat.

804
01:28:24,216 --> 01:28:25,884
(GRUNTING)

805
01:28:28,220 --> 01:28:29,846
We have to go under!

806
01:28:31,932 --> 01:28:33,933
No, I can't breathe
like you!

807
01:28:34,727 --> 01:28:36,561
I'll breathe for both of us.

808
01:28:41,275 --> 01:28:42,901
(GUNS FIRING)

809
01:29:03,380 --> 01:29:04,797
ENOLA: Helen!

810
01:29:07,426 --> 01:29:09,052
Helen, help!

811
01:29:09,136 --> 01:29:13,014
Helen! Helen!

812
01:29:44,004 --> 01:29:45,505
My boat.

813
01:29:55,474 --> 01:29:56,808
Enola.

814
01:29:59,645 --> 01:30:02,730
DEACON: What is this?
Get those chains off her.

815
01:30:02,815 --> 01:30:05,733
What are we,
barbarians here?

816
01:30:05,818 --> 01:30:08,194
Goodness gracious.
Sakes alive.

817
01:30:08,279 --> 01:30:11,072
Come on out of here,
you big, nasty animal, you!

818
01:30:12,449 --> 01:30:14,325
Well, rub-a-dub-dub.

819
01:30:16,537 --> 01:30:19,038
You wanna come over here
and sit on my lap?

820
01:30:20,291 --> 01:30:21,457
No?

821
01:30:23,002 --> 01:30:24,627
How about a cigarette?

822
01:30:26,672 --> 01:30:29,465
Nothing like a good smoke
if you miss your mom.

823
01:30:29,550 --> 01:30:31,509
Never too young to start.

824
01:30:33,012 --> 01:30:36,764
No. Well, I got something
right here I know you'd like.

825
01:30:38,267 --> 01:30:40,143
Like to draw, don't you?

826
01:30:44,648 --> 01:30:46,190
Now, they're yours,

827
01:30:47,526 --> 01:30:51,904
if you help me with
just one problem, all right?

828
01:30:54,199 --> 01:30:57,827
Now, that tattoo
on your back...

829
01:30:59,705 --> 01:31:04,626
I've been told that
that's actually a map?

830
01:31:07,171 --> 01:31:09,464
It's the way to Dryland.

831
01:31:09,548 --> 01:31:11,382
Now we're getting someplace.

832
01:31:13,385 --> 01:31:16,554
Could you teach me
how to read that?

833
01:31:18,974 --> 01:31:20,183
Setback.

834
01:31:23,228 --> 01:31:27,398
Well, have you ever heard any
of your friends talk about it?

835
01:31:27,483 --> 01:31:31,110
You know, like your mommy
or your pet fish?

836
01:31:31,195 --> 01:31:32,654
Did he say anything?

837
01:31:32,738 --> 01:31:35,239
You shouldn't make fun of
him. He wouldn't like it.

838
01:31:35,324 --> 01:31:40,244
Girl, I don't give a shit what
he'd like. He took out my eye.

839
01:31:40,746 --> 01:31:42,205
And if I ever see him again,

840
01:31:42,289 --> 01:31:46,668
I'm gonna cut open his head
and eat his brains!

841
01:31:46,752 --> 01:31:48,961
You think he'd like that?

842
01:31:49,046 --> 01:31:52,924
Can't kill him. He's
even meaner than you are.

843
01:31:53,717 --> 01:31:55,968
But he's not here.

844
01:31:56,053 --> 01:31:58,012
And he ain't coming.

845
01:31:58,097 --> 01:32:00,264
And no one's gonna save you.

846
01:32:01,433 --> 01:32:03,351
He'll come for me. He will.

847
01:32:03,435 --> 01:32:05,978
Then you better tell me
what I wanna know

848
01:32:06,063 --> 01:32:09,607
or he can see what's left
of you in a goddamn jar.

849
01:32:19,952 --> 01:32:22,078
We're going to
die here, aren't we?

850
01:32:42,141 --> 01:32:43,433
Aren't we?

851
01:32:53,235 --> 01:32:55,069
After the atoll,

852
01:32:56,196 --> 01:32:58,322
when I offered
myself to you,

853
01:33:01,493 --> 01:33:03,286
why didn't you take me?

854
01:33:05,789 --> 01:33:07,874
'Cause you didn't
really want me.

855
01:33:10,043 --> 01:33:11,419
Not really.

856
01:35:08,453 --> 01:35:10,121
Have I been asleep long?

857
01:35:16,461 --> 01:35:17,920
I was dreaming.

858
01:35:21,091 --> 01:35:22,383
It's funny.

859
01:35:24,052 --> 01:35:26,137
I always thought
Dryland floated.

860
01:35:26,805 --> 01:35:30,683
That it drifted with the wind.
That's why it was so hard to find.

861
01:35:30,767 --> 01:35:33,019
Why did you believe
in it so much?

862
01:35:34,313 --> 01:35:36,647
Because we weren't
made for the sea.

863
01:35:38,191 --> 01:35:41,444
Got hands and feet.

864
01:35:41,528 --> 01:35:43,279
We're supposed to walk.

865
01:35:47,200 --> 01:35:48,826
I miss her sound.

866
01:35:51,496 --> 01:35:52,663
Don't you?

867
01:35:56,418 --> 01:35:57,793
GREGOR: Helen!

868
01:35:58,837 --> 01:36:01,839
No, no, no.
Helen, up here. Above you!

869
01:36:02,007 --> 01:36:03,841
(GREGOR GIGGLING GIDDILY)
HELEN: Gregor!

870
01:36:09,097 --> 01:36:11,474
Smart thinking,
to burn your boat.

871
01:36:11,558 --> 01:36:15,311
Without the smoke,
I never would have found you.

872
01:36:15,395 --> 01:36:17,521
Who's that with you?

873
01:36:17,606 --> 01:36:20,858
<i>Ichthyus sapien,
is that really you?</i>

874
01:36:21,735 --> 01:36:25,571
I'll throw the rope down. You
catch it. I'll help you climb up.

875
01:36:27,366 --> 01:36:31,452
Helen. Where's Enola?

876
01:36:31,536 --> 01:36:36,499
Smokers took her. They'd have
me, too, if it weren't for him.

877
01:36:36,583 --> 01:36:40,920
My friend, thank you.
Very human of you.

878
01:36:42,297 --> 01:36:44,966
Go grab your things quickly.
Quickly. Come.

879
01:36:53,266 --> 01:36:55,351
GREGOR: You see?
Right down there.

880
01:36:55,435 --> 01:36:58,813
There's several of us survivors.
We'll just start over again.

881
01:37:00,565 --> 01:37:02,149
MAN: You can't ask us
to go get her!

882
01:37:02,234 --> 01:37:03,818
WOMAN 1: Enola's the reason
we're in all this trouble.

883
01:37:03,902 --> 01:37:05,236
WOMAN 2: Forget it!
We're not going.

884
01:37:05,320 --> 01:37:06,904
HELEN: They don't have any
reason to kill her.

885
01:37:06,989 --> 01:37:09,490
They need to find Dryland
just like we do.

886
01:37:09,574 --> 01:37:10,908
GREGOR: Of course.

887
01:37:10,993 --> 01:37:13,661
HELEN: If the Mariner's
willing to go, why not us?

888
01:37:13,745 --> 01:37:15,454
WOMAN 1: If he wants to go,
then let him!

889
01:37:15,539 --> 01:37:18,082
WOMAN 2: We don't want you
here! You or the girl!

890
01:37:19,084 --> 01:37:23,587
Helen. The papers
the Mariner brought you...

891
01:37:23,672 --> 01:37:27,258
It's the same language
the tattoo is written in.

892
01:37:27,342 --> 01:37:28,551
They're numbers.

893
01:37:28,677 --> 01:37:31,721
MAN: Gregor, you're as crazy as she is!
WOMAN 1: We can't stay here!

894
01:37:31,805 --> 01:37:33,014
We're wasting valuable time.

895
01:37:33,098 --> 01:37:35,391
More Smokers will come.
We need to be moving now!

896
01:37:35,475 --> 01:37:39,687
My friends, don't you see we need
this child if we want to find Dryland?

897
01:37:39,771 --> 01:37:41,814
I'm not going.
Nobody is.

898
01:37:41,898 --> 01:37:44,316
HELEN: We've seen
their smoke on the horizon!

899
01:37:44,443 --> 01:37:47,194
Know what that is yet?
GREGOR: The numbers? Yes, I think I do.

900
01:37:47,279 --> 01:37:51,032
It seems to be some ancient
form of geographic location,

901
01:37:51,116 --> 01:37:52,658
latitude, longitude.

902
01:37:52,743 --> 01:37:54,285
It doesn't make sense,
though.

903
01:37:54,369 --> 01:37:57,580
The numbers, they seem to be
backwards or something.

904
01:37:57,664 --> 01:37:59,915
It's upside down.
The world?

905
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:02,585
The poles have
reversed themselves?

906
01:38:02,669 --> 01:38:04,837
I've been mapping
the cities below.

907
01:38:07,466 --> 01:38:11,135
The world wasn't created in a
deluge. It was covered by it.

908
01:38:11,219 --> 01:38:14,346
MAN: That's blasphemy!
No, it's true.

909
01:38:14,431 --> 01:38:19,226
I've seen it with my own eyes. There
is land. It's right under our keel.

910
01:38:19,311 --> 01:38:21,479
None of it's dry anymore.
Some is.

911
01:38:23,106 --> 01:38:27,276
Enola's been there. I know
that now. I saw what she drew.

912
01:38:27,360 --> 01:38:31,489
So that's why he's going
after Enola, to find Dryland.

913
01:38:32,115 --> 01:38:34,158
I don't care about Dryland.

914
01:38:34,242 --> 01:38:37,078
This is ridiculous.
Going after the Smokers?

915
01:38:37,162 --> 01:38:39,080
He doesn't even know which
direction they came from.

916
01:38:39,206 --> 01:38:40,233
MAN: I say let him go.

917
01:38:40,257 --> 01:38:42,416
WOMAN 1: We're better
off without them anyway!

918
01:38:44,086 --> 01:38:46,670
If she's alive,
I'll bring her back to you.

919
01:38:48,256 --> 01:38:52,510
WOMAN 2: Forget the mute-o, Helen. If
he brings her back, they'll kill us!

920
01:38:52,594 --> 01:38:56,597
I can't stand it. We gotta go help
him. I can't ask him to do this alone.

921
01:38:56,681 --> 01:39:00,518
Oh, don't worry, my dear.
You're not going to have to.

922
01:39:06,775 --> 01:39:09,276
You got that tattoo
figured out yet?

923
01:39:09,361 --> 01:39:12,404
It would be easier
if it was flat, big and flat.

924
01:39:12,489 --> 01:39:15,282
It needs to be flat.
What you got in mind?

925
01:39:15,367 --> 01:39:19,662
Well, you cut it off her back.
You stretch it, mount it.

926
01:39:19,788 --> 01:39:23,290
Give us a proper look.
DEACON: Take her down below until I holler.

927
01:39:24,376 --> 01:39:26,293
Saint Joe, we're close.

928
01:39:28,046 --> 01:39:30,256
After centuries of shame.

929
01:39:30,257 --> 01:39:30,297
After centuries of shame.

930
01:40:17,512 --> 01:40:19,930
(PEOPLE SHOUTING IN DISTANCE)

931
01:41:00,764 --> 01:41:03,724
They're getting ugly.
Don't I know it.

932
01:41:04,726 --> 01:41:06,810
Announce me, cousin.

933
01:41:08,980 --> 01:41:10,606
(CROWD CHANTING)

934
01:41:11,233 --> 01:41:12,900
<i>Hey. Here he is.</i>

935
01:41:12,984 --> 01:41:14,777
CROWD: Deacon!

936
01:41:14,861 --> 01:41:17,613
<i>Rise up,
brothers and sisters.</i>

937
01:41:19,157 --> 01:41:22,534
<i>Turn your eyes
and open your hearts</i>

938
01:41:22,619 --> 01:41:24,995
<i>to your humble benefactor,</i>

939
01:41:25,080 --> 01:41:28,791
<i>your spiritual shepherd
and dictator for life,</i>

940
01:41:28,875 --> 01:41:31,627
<i>the Deacon of the 'Deez.</i>

941
01:41:32,420 --> 01:41:34,046
(CROWD CHEERING)

942
01:41:54,484 --> 01:41:56,193
(CROWD CLAMORING)

943
01:42:03,201 --> 01:42:04,785
Deacon!

944
01:42:06,871 --> 01:42:10,791
<i>Children. Children
of the provider.</i>

945
01:42:12,377 --> 01:42:16,380
<i>Citizens of the good ship.
Please, now, hear me speak.</i>

946
01:42:20,427 --> 01:42:23,470
<i>I've had a vision.
MAN: Yeah, we're tired of your visions!</i>

947
01:42:23,555 --> 01:42:25,431
What about the land
you promised us?

948
01:42:25,515 --> 01:42:28,559
ALL: Yeah!

949
01:42:28,643 --> 01:42:31,186
<i>DEACON: Oh, ye of little faith.</i>

950
01:42:31,271 --> 01:42:33,522
<i>I've had a vision so great</i>

951
01:42:33,606 --> 01:42:36,984
<i>that as it came to me, I wept.</i>

952
01:42:37,068 --> 01:42:39,403
<i>And in this splenditudinous
figment of wonder...</i>

953
01:42:39,487 --> 01:42:40,738
Is that Bone's ride?

954
01:42:40,822 --> 01:42:43,365
<i>Is that blood on the seat?
... I saw.</i>

955
01:42:43,450 --> 01:42:47,161
<i>And you know what it is
that I saw.</i>

956
01:42:47,245 --> 01:42:51,498
<i>I saw the land.</i>

957
01:42:51,708 --> 01:42:53,125
(ALL CHEERING)

958
01:43:01,760 --> 01:43:03,093
(MUFFLED SCREAMING)

959
01:43:16,649 --> 01:43:18,400
(CROWD CHANTING)

960
01:43:24,157 --> 01:43:25,532
Horse, that you?

961
01:43:26,534 --> 01:43:28,285
(ENGINE REVVING)

962
01:43:30,955 --> 01:43:34,208
Look, just bring it in slow,
okay? I don't have all day.

963
01:43:34,292 --> 01:43:35,542
(ENGINE REVVING)

964
01:43:36,669 --> 01:43:37,878
Horse!

965
01:43:37,962 --> 01:43:39,546
Just bring it up slow.

966
01:43:41,424 --> 01:43:44,218
I said slow! No, Horse!

967
01:43:49,015 --> 01:43:52,351
Shouldn't be doing that.
You're gonna get in trouble.

968
01:43:53,812 --> 01:43:55,312
That's right.

969
01:43:55,397 --> 01:43:56,730
You're not afraid.

970
01:43:56,815 --> 01:43:59,566
You've got your pet freak
coming to rescue you.

971
01:43:59,651 --> 01:44:02,236
He's not a freak,
and he can take you anytime.

972
01:44:04,114 --> 01:44:08,367
He's killed dozens of people, and he
doesn't have any mercy or anything.

973
01:44:08,451 --> 01:44:10,536
He even kills little girls.

974
01:44:12,455 --> 01:44:13,997
Haven't we all?

975
01:44:15,583 --> 01:44:16,708
Shit.

976
01:44:18,002 --> 01:44:19,128
It's Horse.

977
01:44:21,506 --> 01:44:23,799
Find him. Find him!
Come on!

978
01:44:26,052 --> 01:44:28,720
<i>Yes! Yes! Yes!</i>

979
01:44:28,805 --> 01:44:32,266
Come on.
ENOLA: Getting nervous, aren't you?

980
01:44:36,396 --> 01:44:37,855
I don't get nervous.

981
01:44:39,691 --> 01:44:42,359
<i>DEACON: And if there's
a river, we'll dam it.</i>

982
01:44:42,444 --> 01:44:46,321
<i>And if there's a tree,
we'll ram it.</i>

983
01:44:47,157 --> 01:44:49,867
<i>'Cause I'm talking
progress here.</i>

984
01:44:49,951 --> 01:44:53,162
<i>Yes, sir.
I'm talking development.</i>

985
01:44:53,246 --> 01:44:54,288
Yeah!

986
01:44:54,372 --> 01:44:59,126
<i>For we shall suck and savor
the sweet flavor</i>

987
01:44:59,210 --> 01:45:01,879
<i>of Dryland.</i>

988
01:45:02,172 --> 01:45:03,881
ENOLA: Face is all red.

989
01:45:05,550 --> 01:45:08,760
Helen said with anybody
that has a face that red,

990
01:45:08,845 --> 01:45:11,180
he's had too much sun
or too much to drink.

991
01:45:11,306 --> 01:45:15,267
All right, that's it. I'm gonna
burn your face. Almost time.

992
01:45:16,811 --> 01:45:18,020
What's up?

993
01:45:18,104 --> 01:45:20,230
We're just talking
about her friend.

994
01:45:21,649 --> 01:45:24,943
NORD: What's his name?
ENOLA: He doesn't have a name.

995
01:45:25,028 --> 01:45:27,070
So death can't find him.

996
01:45:27,405 --> 01:45:28,739
(MOCKING ENOLA'S TONE)

997
01:45:32,702 --> 01:45:35,245
Doesn't have a home
or people to care for.

998
01:45:36,414 --> 01:45:40,000
He's not afraid of anything,
men least of all.

999
01:45:40,084 --> 01:45:43,337
<i>He's fast and strong
like a big wind.</i>

1000
01:45:43,421 --> 01:45:47,174
<i>He can hear 100 miles
and see 100 miles underwater.</i>

1001
01:45:47,258 --> 01:45:49,760
<i>He can hide in the shadow
of the noon sun.</i>

1002
01:45:50,595 --> 01:45:54,598
He could be right behind you, and you
won't even know it till you're dead!

1003
01:45:56,935 --> 01:45:58,227
Give it a rest.

1004
01:46:00,313 --> 01:46:02,356
He'll come for me. He will.

1005
01:46:02,440 --> 01:46:05,067
<i>DEACON: We are the reason.
Yeah?</i>

1006
01:46:05,151 --> 01:46:08,320
<i>Well, I hope he does.
The Lord's creation.</i>

1007
01:46:10,406 --> 01:46:12,115
Remember you said that.

1008
01:46:12,951 --> 01:46:14,660
<i>DEACON: Look at us.</i>

1009
01:46:15,620 --> 01:46:18,038
<i>Look at us here today.
Yeah!</i>

1010
01:46:18,122 --> 01:46:21,833
<i>Dryland is not
just our destination,</i>

1011
01:46:22,460 --> 01:46:25,003
<i>but it is our destiny!</i>

1012
01:46:25,171 --> 01:46:26,672
(ALL CHEERING)

1013
01:46:33,638 --> 01:46:36,139
Find him. Find him!

1014
01:46:36,224 --> 01:46:40,936
<i>How can I find this
glorious place?</i>

1015
01:46:41,479 --> 01:46:43,272
<i>And he said to me,</i>

1016
01:46:44,399 --> 01:46:48,652
<i>"A child shall lead you. "</i>

1017
01:46:49,821 --> 01:46:52,072
<i>A child.</i>

1018
01:46:52,156 --> 01:46:54,491
<i>And behold!
It's time.</i>

1019
01:46:55,076 --> 01:46:57,911
<i>The instrument
of our salvation!</i>

1020
01:46:59,289 --> 01:47:00,956
(CROWD CHEERING)

1021
01:47:05,003 --> 01:47:07,588
<i>Our guide in the wild.</i>

1022
01:47:07,672 --> 01:47:10,090
<i>Our beacon in the darkness.</i>

1023
01:47:10,174 --> 01:47:12,092
Turn her around, Nord.

1024
01:47:12,176 --> 01:47:14,970
<i>She has shown me the path!</i>

1025
01:47:19,225 --> 01:47:22,894
<i>And before the holy-most
moment is upon us,</i>

1026
01:47:22,979 --> 01:47:26,690
<i>let's sacrifice one
to old Saint Joe.</i>

1027
01:47:29,694 --> 01:47:32,946
<i>And let's get this tub of shit
up to speed.</i>

1028
01:47:39,495 --> 01:47:40,579
Yeah.

1029
01:47:58,264 --> 01:47:59,931
(MEN CHATTERING)

1030
01:48:19,118 --> 01:48:20,786
Pull!

1031
01:48:20,870 --> 01:48:23,955
Pull!

1032
01:48:24,082 --> 01:48:26,249
Pull.
Pull!

1033
01:48:33,591 --> 01:48:34,883
(MEN GRUNTING)

1034
01:48:45,978 --> 01:48:49,272
So which way we rowing?
I don't have a goddamn clue.

1035
01:48:49,357 --> 01:48:51,066
Don't worry.

1036
01:48:51,150 --> 01:48:54,319
They'll row for a month before
they figure out I'm faking it.

1037
01:48:54,404 --> 01:48:57,197
But why?

1038
01:48:57,281 --> 01:48:58,782
Oh, you want details.

1039
01:48:58,866 --> 01:49:00,242
Well, I'm not
telling these savages

1040
01:49:00,326 --> 01:49:02,536
that we haven't
solved the map yet.

1041
01:49:02,620 --> 01:49:03,995
I promised them results,

1042
01:49:04,080 --> 01:49:07,666
and I'll get them if I gotta
cut them out of her goddamn back.

1043
01:49:10,253 --> 01:49:12,796
Who is that?
Who is that?

1044
01:49:14,340 --> 01:49:16,800
Maybe one of
the spare rowers?

1045
01:49:24,475 --> 01:49:26,393
<i>Why aren't you rowing?</i>

1046
01:49:35,820 --> 01:49:37,988
It's him.
It is him.

1047
01:49:39,198 --> 01:49:41,366
You guys are in
so much trouble.

1048
01:49:44,746 --> 01:49:47,956
<i>Well, I'll be damned.
It's the gentleman guppy.</i>

1049
01:49:49,167 --> 01:49:51,710
<i>You know, he's like a turd
that won't flush.</i>

1050
01:49:52,879 --> 01:49:54,296
I want the girl.

1051
01:49:58,217 --> 01:50:02,888
<i>You know, I thought
you were stupid, friend.</i>

1052
01:50:04,015 --> 01:50:06,391
<i>But I underestimated you.</i>

1053
01:50:06,476 --> 01:50:09,770
<i>You're a total
freaking retard.</i>

1054
01:50:09,979 --> 01:50:11,438
(DEACON LAUGHS)

1055
01:50:12,690 --> 01:50:16,401
I want the girl. That's all.

1056
01:50:16,486 --> 01:50:20,530
<i>Well, what on this screwed-up earth
makes you think you're gonna get her?</i>

1057
01:50:28,831 --> 01:50:30,582
You know what this is?

1058
01:50:35,296 --> 01:50:37,881
I drop it, you burn.

1059
01:50:38,216 --> 01:50:41,718
<i>We all burn.
Now, wait, wait, wait, wait.</i>

1060
01:50:43,095 --> 01:50:45,722
<i>Let's not do
anything rash here.</i>

1061
01:50:45,807 --> 01:50:48,308
<i>I mean, are you sure
she's worth all this?</i>

1062
01:50:48,392 --> 01:50:51,561
<i>She never does stop talking.
She never shuts up.</i>

1063
01:50:52,313 --> 01:50:55,398
<i>I noticed.
What is it, then?</i>

1064
01:50:55,983 --> 01:50:57,442
<i>It's the map.</i>

1065
01:50:58,402 --> 01:50:59,736
She's my friend.

1066
01:51:01,197 --> 01:51:04,199
<i>Golly gee. A single tear
rolls down my cheek.</i>

1067
01:51:04,283 --> 01:51:06,743
<i>I mean, you're gonna
die for your friend?</i>

1068
01:51:08,621 --> 01:51:10,121
If it comes to that.

1069
01:51:12,500 --> 01:51:14,334
He's bluffing.
I'll kill him.

1070
01:51:14,418 --> 01:51:16,586
He's not bluffing.
He never bluffs.

1071
01:51:16,671 --> 01:51:17,963
Shut up!

1072
01:51:20,341 --> 01:51:24,135
<i>I don't think you're gonna
drop that torch, my friend.</i>

1073
01:51:25,012 --> 01:51:28,223
<i>Why not?
Because you're not crazy.</i>

1074
01:51:32,103 --> 01:51:33,854
<i>No!</i>

1075
01:51:41,612 --> 01:51:43,321
Oh, thank God.

1076
01:51:43,865 --> 01:51:45,615
(MAN SCREAMING)

1077
01:51:56,043 --> 01:51:57,460
(MACHINE GUN FIRING)

1078
01:52:02,133 --> 01:52:04,050
Look out!

1079
01:52:04,135 --> 01:52:05,552
That's him!

1080
01:52:06,762 --> 01:52:08,680
Let's go, God damn it!

1081
01:52:12,143 --> 01:52:14,978
Don't just stand there!
Kill something!

1082
01:52:18,024 --> 01:52:19,774
Was this your big vision?

1083
01:52:28,868 --> 01:52:32,287
I've always wanted
to drive this monster.

1084
01:52:35,666 --> 01:52:38,001
There he is! There he is!

1085
01:52:42,173 --> 01:52:43,924
That's him! There!

1086
01:52:46,594 --> 01:52:48,178
(SCREAMS)

1087
01:52:57,647 --> 01:53:00,523
ENOLA: Let me go! Let go!

1088
01:53:10,993 --> 01:53:12,118
Let go!

1089
01:53:16,582 --> 01:53:19,334
Let me go! Let go!
DEACON: Pilot!

1090
01:53:19,418 --> 01:53:21,544
Pilot!
Let me go!

1091
01:53:21,629 --> 01:53:23,296
Let go!

1092
01:53:23,381 --> 01:53:25,882
Let go!

1093
01:53:28,719 --> 01:53:31,721
You should've stayed
underwater.

1094
01:53:32,264 --> 01:53:33,974
(CLICKING)

1095
01:53:40,982 --> 01:53:44,734
Now, you know how to fly an
airplane? You're lucky I do.

1096
01:53:44,819 --> 01:53:45,819
(SCREAMS)

1097
01:53:45,903 --> 01:53:48,405
Now, you stop it!
You behave yourself, now!

1098
01:55:13,532 --> 01:55:15,200
(EXPLOSION)

1099
01:55:20,873 --> 01:55:22,540
HELEN: It's going down.

1100
01:55:22,625 --> 01:55:27,170
You can see it sinking. Gregor,
bring us right down on top of it.

1101
01:55:27,671 --> 01:55:29,756
Hurry, Gregor!
GREGOR: Don't worry.

1102
01:55:29,840 --> 01:55:30,840
Hurry.

1103
01:55:44,980 --> 01:55:47,232
Enola! Up here!

1104
01:55:47,316 --> 01:55:48,608
Grab on!

1105
01:55:54,073 --> 01:55:55,824
GREGOR: Are they on yet?

1106
01:55:59,453 --> 01:56:01,663
GREGOR: What is it?
HELEN: I don't know!

1107
01:56:13,717 --> 01:56:17,929
Got you! I'm gonna rip
your cute little lungs out.

1108
01:56:22,935 --> 01:56:24,394
(SCREAMING)

1109
01:56:27,982 --> 01:56:29,315
(GRUNTING)

1110
01:56:33,279 --> 01:56:35,321
Pull! Almost.

1111
01:56:43,247 --> 01:56:44,247
(EXPLOSION)

1112
01:57:14,612 --> 01:57:15,653
(SCREAMS)

1113
01:57:15,738 --> 01:57:18,489
HELEN: No! Enola!
No!

1114
01:57:20,743 --> 01:57:22,994
Saint Joe. Hole in one.

1115
01:57:23,078 --> 01:57:24,162
Helen!

1116
01:57:24,246 --> 01:57:25,830
No! Enola!

1117
01:57:26,081 --> 01:57:27,373
GREGOR: Helen!

1118
01:57:27,791 --> 01:57:29,792
Hey! Hey!

1119
01:57:36,467 --> 01:57:39,761
HELEN: Oh, no! Quick!
What are we gonna do?

1120
01:57:43,599 --> 01:57:44,933
Tie this off.

1121
01:57:49,688 --> 01:57:51,648
Hurry! Tie it!

1122
01:57:57,238 --> 01:57:58,404
HELEN: Got it!

1123
01:57:58,781 --> 01:57:59,989
(SCREAMS)

1124
01:58:02,993 --> 01:58:04,702
(SCREAMING)

1125
01:58:12,544 --> 01:58:13,920
Get them up now.

1126
01:58:21,845 --> 01:58:23,513
(GREGOR LAUGHING)

1127
01:58:25,599 --> 01:58:28,268
GREGOR: Of course! North is south. What?

1128
01:58:28,352 --> 01:58:29,477
South is north.

1129
01:58:29,561 --> 01:58:30,853
I was swimming.

1130
01:58:31,855 --> 01:58:34,107
I saw.
GREGOR: My friends.

1131
01:58:34,191 --> 01:58:36,109
My friends, look.

1132
01:58:36,193 --> 01:58:37,944
If I take a bearing
from the sun

1133
01:58:38,028 --> 01:58:40,863
and reverse the coordinates
on Enola's back,

1134
01:58:43,117 --> 01:58:45,201
then Dryland is that way!

1135
01:59:55,397 --> 01:59:57,023
(SEAGULL CAWING)

1136
02:00:10,496 --> 02:00:11,954
(SEAGULL CAWING)

1137
02:00:47,825 --> 02:00:49,325
(HELEN LAUGHING)

1138
02:00:49,576 --> 02:00:50,910
It's fresh!

1139
02:00:50,994 --> 02:00:53,663
All of this! It's fresh!

1140
02:00:53,831 --> 02:00:55,498
(HELEN WHOOPING)

1141
02:00:58,293 --> 02:00:59,877
(ALL LAUGHING)

1142
02:01:09,555 --> 02:01:11,514
I found something!

1143
02:01:19,565 --> 02:01:21,274
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

1144
02:01:24,820 --> 02:01:27,196
Maybe we should put them
under the dirt.

1145
02:01:28,365 --> 02:01:30,116
I think it was their way.

1146
02:01:32,536 --> 02:01:34,662
They must have known
they were dying.

1147
02:01:34,788 --> 02:01:35,913
(HELEN LAUGHING)

1148
02:01:57,102 --> 02:01:58,394
(WINDING)

1149
02:02:01,273 --> 02:02:02,857
(MUSIC PLAYING)

1150
02:02:05,360 --> 02:02:06,652
I'm home.

1151
02:02:18,123 --> 02:02:20,750
Where's the Mariner?
He was outside.

1152
02:02:25,923 --> 02:02:28,633
Wait. Wait!
I want to show you this.

1153
02:02:29,218 --> 02:02:30,843
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

1154
02:02:37,392 --> 02:02:39,227
(HORSES NEIGHING)

1155
02:02:45,442 --> 02:02:46,859
(HORSES GALLOPING)

1156
02:03:00,999 --> 02:03:02,667
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

1157
02:03:41,331 --> 02:03:44,000
First time in your life
you got nothing to say?

1158
02:03:58,515 --> 02:04:00,224
Enola, I have to go now.

1159
02:04:01,393 --> 02:04:03,144
But you came back for me.

1160
02:04:05,230 --> 02:04:06,522
I like you.

1161
02:04:12,237 --> 02:04:13,863
Why are you leaving?

1162
02:04:17,367 --> 02:04:21,037
I don't belong here. I belong
out there. You belong here.

1163
02:04:23,957 --> 02:04:26,584
It's too strange here.
It doesn't move right.

1164
02:04:27,377 --> 02:04:30,254
Helen said that
it's only landsickness.

1165
02:04:31,131 --> 02:04:34,008
We're all feeling it.
It'll go away soon.

1166
02:04:34,843 --> 02:04:36,385
It's more than that.

